{"parentUuid":"5551bea3-e45b-40a3-b4ca-fdbce24e517a","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"ee6c7d20-9584-49a7-95fa-f8a6a436aeb8","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FsPABKVjwZcJd19nn2GBHG","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"No prior reviews on file. Now I have enough context to write the review. The user wants it posted as a final response (the harness says don't write report files), so I'll synthesize the findings.\n\nLet me also verify one more thing — the behavior when `main..HEAD` returns a range that's way beyond the `--since` window. The `--since` filter on `git log` prunes further. So even a deep range is harmless. OK.\n\nOne last thing: the pre-existing `reachableCheckpointIDsOnHEAD` (line 213) is now a one-line wrapper around `reachableCheckpointIDsInRange(ctx, repoRoot, branchLocalRevRange(ctx, repoRoot), since)` — which is the same thing `enumerateRepoCandidates` line 157 does directly. So `reachableCheckpointIDsOnHEAD` is called only from the test at line 549. I.e., it's a test-only helper now, which is why the test still passes after the `branchLocalRevRange` indirection. Minor dead code smell.\n\nAlso: the test `TestReachableCheckpointIDsOnHEAD_...` (line 533) uses a shim `git` binary and it asserts that `git log` args contain `--grep` and `Entire-Checkpoint:` and `--since=2026-04-01T12:30:00Z`. But the shim-produced args also now contain the rev-range computed from `branchLocalRevRange(ctx, \"/tmp/repo\")`. That function invokes `symbolic-ref` and `rev-parse` against `/tmp/repo` — which also go through the shim because the shim is on PATH. The shim script just prints the same response for ANY invocation: `printf 'subject\\n\\nEntire-Checkpoint: testCheckpointID\\000'`. So `symbolic-ref --quiet refs/remotes/origin/HEAD` returns that output with exit 0. Then `branchLocalRevRange` does:\n\n```go\nif out, ok := runGitOutput(...); ok {\n    ref := strings.TrimSpace(out)\n    if strings.HasPrefix(ref, \"refs/remotes/\") {\n        return strings.TrimPrefix(ref, \"refs/remotes/\")\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThe output is `subject\\n\\nEntire-Checkpoint: testCheckpointID` — `HasPrefix` is false. Then it falls into the candidate list: `rev-parse --verify --quiet origin/main` — shim returns `subject\\n\\nEntire-Checkpoint: testCheckpointID`, exit 0 (not the real rev-parse behavior, which would fail on unknown refs). So `branchLocalRevRange` returns `\"origin/main..HEAD\"`. The test asserts `--grep` and `--since=...` are in args — both pass, but the test is incidentally relying on shim pseudo-behavior.\n\nMore importantly: the shim writes args once to `TEST_GIT_ARGS_FILE` but the shim is called multiple times (once for `symbolic-ref`, potentially multiple `rev-parse` attempts, finally for `log`). Each overwrites the file. Because `log` is the last call, the file ends up containing the `log` args. Lucky but brittle — if anyone ever reorders `branchLocalRevRange` to call something after `log`, this test breaks silently. Worth flagging.\n\nAlso, the shim will be invoked as `git symbolic-ref --quiet refs/remotes/origin/HEAD` — it overwrites args to:\n```\nsymbolic-ref\n--quiet\nrefs/remotes/origin/HEAD\n```\n\nThen `git rev-parse --verify --quiet origin/main` → rewrites args file. Since the prefix check fails for `subject...`, it always falls through. Ultimately the `log` invocation is the last one and its args land in the file.\n\n---\n\nNow I have enough. Let me write the review.\n\n---\n\n# Code Review: `entire-dispatch-codex`\n\nBranch vs. `main`: ~5.1k LOC added, all net-new (dispatch is a new command). My review focuses on the three commits flagged in the request plus anything else that surfaced.\n\n---\n\n## 🔴 Must-fix\n\n**1. `defaultBranchRef` silently picks a stale `main` over the real default.**\n`cmd/entire/cli/dispatch/mode_local.go:259-272`. When `refs/remotes/origin/HEAD` is not set (common on CI clones, manual `git clone --no-single-branch`, worktrees created with certain options), the fallback is hardcoded to `origin/main`, `origin/master`, `main`, `master`. A repo whose real default is `develop`, `trunk`, `next`, `release`, etc. falls back to whatever stale `main`/`master` ref happens to exist locally. Real-world consequence: on a `develop`-based repo where someone once fetched `main` long ago, every feature-branch dispatch will exclude the wrong baseline and include a lot of old `develop` history as \"feature-branch work,\" silently.\n\nMitigations:\n- Before falling back, try `git config --get init.defaultBranch` and `git remote show origin | grep 'HEAD branch'`.\n- Or just bail out (`return \"\"`, leave the old `HEAD` behavior) when `origin/HEAD` is unset — at least the regression is \"no filtering\" not \"wrong filtering.\"\n- Log (not error) when you fall back, so users can see what base got picked.\n\nThere is no test for this case. Add one with a repo where `master` exists but `origin/HEAD` points at something else — today the code would pick `master` over the real default.\n\n**2. Cloud `--repos` cap is only enforced at the CLI input layer.**\n`cmd/entire/cli/dispatch/options.go:26-28` enforces the 5-repo cap in `ResolveOptions`. `runServer` (`mode_cloud.go:15-77`) does not re-check. Anyone calling `dispatch.Run` programmatically with `Options{Mode: ModeServer, RepoPaths: tenRepos}` bypasses the cap and the server receives a 10-repo payload. Not end-of-the-world because there's only one caller today, but the cap is a policy decision that belongs on the constructor / request builder or the server, not on a flag parser. The request mentioned this — yes, it should also be enforced server-side. Client-side enforcement alone is a soft limit that will erode.\n\n**3. `REDACTED` is fragile under the new rev-range indirection.**\n`mode_local_test.go:533-568`. The shim `git` binary always writes to the same args file, so whichever git invocation runs last wins. With `d5cb2b9af`, `branchLocalRevRange` now invokes `git symbolic-ref`, then possibly several `git rev-parse`, then `git log`. The test still passes by accident because `log` happens to be last. Any future reordering silently breaks the assertion (the file will contain `rev-parse` args, not `log` args — both happen to lack `--grep` and `--since=`, so the test would fail loudly, but for the wrong reason). Either:\n- Have the shim append, not overwrite (`>>` instead of `>`), then grep the concatenated output.\n- Or branch on `$1` (the git subcommand) and only write when `$1 == \"log\"`.\n\nRight now the test prints \"list HEAD checkpoint trailers\" assertions that are invisible to anyone reading the shim.\n\n---\n\n## 🟡 Should-fix\n\n**4. `--all-branches` behavior change is user-visible and undocumented in `--help`.**\nPreviously `--all-branches` skipped the branch filter entirely; now it enumerates local branches and filters to that set (`mode_local.go:138-154`, `312-332`). Three consequences for real users:\n- A branch renamed (`git branch -m old new`) after checkpoints were taken → all \"old\" checkpoints disappear, silently.\n- A branch deleted (merged and cleaned up, or just stale) → its checkpoints disappear even if the commits are still reachable from other branches.\n- Checkpoints recorded with `summary.Branch = \"HEAD\"` or detached-HEAD placeholders get filtered out.\n\nThe flag description at `cmd/entire/cli/dispatch.go:73` still says \"include all local branches (--local only)\" — users will read that as \"include everything with a local branch reference,\" which is ambiguous about deleted branches. Tighten to e.g. \"include checkpoints whose branch still exists locally.\"\n\n**5. `APIBranches`, `Title`, `Voice *string`, `Totals`, `Warnings`, `APIBranches.UnmarshalJSON` on the response are all dead code.**\n`cloud.go:65-146`. `apiToDispatch` (`mode_cloud.go:79-126`) reads only `Window`, `CoveredRepos`, `Repos`, `GeneratedMarkdown`, `GeneratedText`. Everything else in the response struct is declared purely so the decoder has somewhere to put bytes that are already ignored thanks to dropping `DisallowUnknownFields`. Specifically:\n- The `APIBranches.UnmarshalJSON` custom decoder (20+ lines handling the `[\"main\"]` / `\"all\"` / `null` trichotomy) is never consumed.\n- `TestCloudClient_CreateDispatch_AcceptsBranchesResponseField`, `TestCloudClient_CreateDispatch_AcceptsAllBranchesSentinelInResponseField`, `TestCloudClient_CreateDispatch_AcceptsVoiceResponseField` exist only to test the decoder, not any behavior the CLI depends on. These are testing-the-test.\n\nRecommendation: delete `APIBranches`, `Title`, `Voice`, `Totals`, `Warnings`, and the three response-field tests. If we later consume them, re-add. Forward-compat is already guaranteed by the `DisallowUnknownFields` removal.\n\n**6. Dropping `DisallowUnknownFields` is correct, but the `TruncatedCount` field is untested.**\n`cloud.go:122`. The commit message says \"add the missing TruncatedCount\" but the new `TestCloudClient_CreateDispatch_IgnoresUnknownResponseFields` only asserts that one completely-unrelated unknown field doesn't break the decoder — it doesn't verify that `TruncatedCount` actually deserializes. Trivial fix: `TestCloudClient_CreateDispatch_DecodesTruncatedCount` asserting `got.Warnings.TruncatedCount == 3`. Given points 5 — if you're deleting `Warnings`, skip this.\n\n**7. `runServer` always ships `voice: \"neutral\"` when the user passes no voice.**\n`mode_cloud.go:65`: `Voice: resolvedDispatchVoicePreference(opts.Voice)`. `resolvedDispatchVoicePreference(\"\")` → `\"neutral\"` (generate.go:209-215). That means every default cloud request tells the server \"I want the neutral voice\" even if the user is fine with the server default. If the server ever adds a better default, CLI users don't get it. Prefer omitting `voice` from the request when the user didn't set one (lean on `omitempty`) and only pass non-empty values.\n\n**8. `reachableCheckpointIDsOnHEAD` is a test-only wrapper now.**\n`mode_local.go:213-215`. Production callers in `enumerateRepoCandidates` moved to `reachableCheckpointIDsInRange(..., branchLocalRevRange(...), ...)` (line 157). The wrapper exists only for the shim test (`REDACTED`). Either inline into the test or point the test at `reachableCheckpointIDsInRange` directly and delete the wrapper.\n\n**9. `REDACTED` is environment-dependent.**\n`mode_local_test.go:419-478`. Relies on go-git's `PlainInit` defaulting to `master` (otherwise the fallback list `origin/main, origin/master, main, master` might not find `master`). If the underlying default changes (go-git v7, or a host with `init.defaultBranch=main` in its repo config), the test still passes because `main` is in the fallback list too. OK today. But: the test never checks that `my-feature`'s checkpoint IS in the output — only that the main checkpoint isn't. Add an explicit assertion that at least one bullet has text `testLocalFallbackText`, otherwise a pure \"no repos\" bug would pass.\n\n**10. New `glamour v0.6.0` dependency is ~2 years behind latest.**\n`go.mod`. The project already uses `lipgloss v1.1.0` (2025-era) and `bubbletea v1.3.10`. Pulling in `glamour v0.6.0` with its own `chroma` + `douceur` transitive pins risks version drift. If the TUI rendering is non-critical (it's just `renderTerminalMarkdown`), verify there's no cleaner path via `lipgloss` or bump glamour to something contemporaneous.\n\n**11. Cloud payload test doesn't actually cover the post-MarshalJSON shape.**\n`cloud_test.go:69-101` (`TestCloudClient_CreateDispatch_OmitsBranchesAndOrgsFromPayload`) verifies `orgs` and `branches` are absent, which is good. But it doesn't verify which fields ARE present — e.g., `repos`, `since`, `until`, `generate`, and `voice` when set. With the custom `MarshalJSON` gone, if someone accidentally renames `Since` to `SinceAt` the struct tag breaks silently. Pin the exact payload keys in at least one test (and reject any unknown keys, inverting the current approach).\n\n---\n\n## 🟢 Nit\n\n**12. `branchLocalRevRange` swallows `defaultBranchRef` errors entirely.**\n`mode_local.go:248-254`. No logging on fallback. Users running against a repo with an unusual default branch have no way to know the filter defaulted to `HEAD` — they'll just see \"too many checkpoints.\" A single `logging.Debug` call with the chosen base would help.\n\n**13. `runGitOutput` treats all non-zero exits as \"ref doesn't exist\".**\n`mode_local.go:274-281`. A `.git/index.lock` or permission error looks identical to \"no such ref.\" Not actionable for a nit, but worth a comment.\n\n**14. `findCommitSubjectByCheckpoint` uses `git log --all` which includes `refs/remotes/*`.**\n`mode_local.go:334-345`. On repos with multiple remotes, this may pick up a stale subject from a rewritten commit on another fork. Narrow to `--branches --remotes` or just `HEAD`-reachable? Marginal.\n\n**15. `Options.RepoPaths` does double duty.**\nIn cloud mode the field holds org/repo slugs; in local mode it holds filesystem paths. Today `ResolveOptions` rejects `--repos` + `--local`, so the two never mix, but the field name implies filesystem. Rename to `Repos` or split into `RepoSlugs`/`RepoFSPaths`.\n\n**16. `firstAt` / `lastAt` asymmetry.**\n`mode_local.go:409-430`. `firstAt` skips zero-valued `CreatedAt`, `lastAt` doesn't (`time.Time{}.After(time.Time{})` is false, so it's still correct — but a future reader has to prove that). Either skip in both or neither, for symmetry.\n\n**17. Test names overclaim in subtle ways.**\n`TestLocalMode_AllBranchesRestrictsToLocalBranches` (line 480) asserts the \"should not appear\" bullet is absent but doesn't positively assert the feature-a checkpoint IS present. If `seedCommittedCheckpoint` quietly broke, the test would still pass.\n\n**18. `--repos` flag help says \"cloud repo slugs.\"**\n`dispatch.go:74`. Clear for the cloud case. But since `--repos` is rejected with `--local`, maybe worth saying \"cloud mode only\" explicitly to save a support ticket.\n\n**19. No test for `--org` flag removal.**\nA test of the form `TestDispatchCmd_OrgFlagIsNotRegistered` that calls `cmd.Flags().Lookup(\"org\")` and asserts nil would prevent accidental re-addition. Maybe overkill.\n\n---\n\n## Things verified / done well\n\n- **Prompt-injection posture is solid.** The local-mode generation prompt at `generate.go:47-68` explicitly names repo names, branch names, commit messages, bullets, warnings, and voice preference as untrusted and tells the model not to follow them. The `<dispatch_data>`/`<voice_preference>` tags are escaped case-insensitively (`dispatchPromptTagPattern` at `generate.go:105`), and the voice string is additionally stripped of control chars + BiDi overrides + zero-width chars (`generate.go:194-207`). This is above the norm.\n- **Bearer-token handling is clean.** Token is only read via `auth.LookupCurrentToken`, only sent to the configured base URL, never logged. No obvious exfil path. The `StatusUnauthorized` branch returns a user-friendly error without echoing the token.\n- **Forward-compat decoder change is the right call.** Strictness on clients + a rolling server = guaranteed CLI break whenever the server adds a field. The tradeoff (silently-ignored response fields) is strictly better than the old tradeoff (silent 100% failure). The risk of masking real server-side bugs is low because the fields in question (warnings, totals) are cosmetic, and the CLI still validates presence of what it uses (`dispatch.GeneratedText` at `mode_cloud.go:73-75`).\n- **Parallelism in `runLocal` is textbook `errgroup` usage.** `groupCtx` is threaded through, mutex-guarded slice append, `group.Wait()` before consuming. Clean.\n- **`localBranchNames` correctly excludes the `entire/` reserved prefix.** `mode_local.go:312-332` + `checkpoint/temporary.go:34-35`. It is not a legitimate concern for a user-named branch: the checkpoint strategy uses `entire/` aggressively for shadow branches and `entire/checkpoints/v1` — collisions would already break checkpointing regardless of dispatch. Safe to filter.\n- **Custom `MarshalJSON` removal is clean.** No partial states left behind; payload-shape test (`cloud_test.go:69-101`) asserts the right negatives.\n- **`ResolveOptions` validation ordering is right.** Rejects `--repos + --local` before checking cap, so you don't get a confusing \"at most 5 repos\" error when the real problem is mode conflict.\n\n---\n\n## Verdict\n\n**REQUEST CHANGES** — the default-branch resolution (#1) is a real silent-wrong-answer bug on a non-`main`/`master` default, and the test fragility (#3) hides it. Those two plus the server-side cap (#2) need addressing. Everything else is should-fix or nit and can be rolled into a follow-up. Once #1–#3 are in I'd approve.\n\nEstimated effort: **~2–3 hours** for #1 (add `git config` + `remote show` probe, new test), **30 min** for #2 (server-side enforcement is separate, but add the client-side defense-in-depth in `runServer`), **15 min** for #3 (make shim append).\n\n---\n\nFiles referenced:\n- `/Users/alisha/Projects/wt/cli/entire-dispatch-codex/cmd/entire/cli/dispatch/mode_local.go`\n- `/Users/alisha/Projects/wt/cli/entire-dispatch-codex/cmd/entire/cli/dispatch/mode_local_test.go`\n- `/Users/alisha/Projects/wt/cli/entire-dispatch-codex/cmd/entire/cli/dispatch/cloud.go`\n- `/Users/alisha/Projects/wt/cli/entire-dispatch-codex/cmd/entire/cli/dispatch/cloud_test.go`\n- `/Users/alisha/Projects/wt/cli/entire-dispatch-codex/cmd/entire/cli/dispatch/mode_cloud.go`\n- `/Users/alisha/Projects/wt/cli/entire-dispatch-codex/cmd/entire/cli/dispatch/mode_cloud_test.go`\n- `/Users/alisha/Projects/wt/cli/entire-dispatch-codex/cmd/entire/cli/dispatch/options.go`\n- `/Users/alisha/Projects/wt/cli/entire-dispatch-codex/cmd/entire/cli/dispatch/generate.go`\n- `/Users/alisha/Projects/wt/cli/entire-dispatch-codex/cmd/entire/cli/dispatch.go`\n- `/Users/alisha/Projects/wt/cli/entire-dispatch-codex/cmd/entire/cli/dispatch_wizard.go`\n- `/Users/alisha/Projects/wt/cli/entire-d